# M31-specific pipeline defaults, with the measured reason for each.
#
# run_pipeline.sh sources this file (override the path with PIPELINE_CONFIG=..., or PIPELINE_CONFIG=/dev/null to
# skip it). The M31_*_ARGS below are passed to each stage BEFORE any QA_ARGS / DEBAND_ARGS / FLAT_ARGS /
# STACK_ARGS / POST_ARGS you set in the environment. Every stage parses its flags left to right with the last
# one winning, so your own flags (and combine_sessions.sh's --weight noise) still override these.
#
# The values restate the scripts' built-in defaults; they are collected here so the M31 tuning is visible in
# one place. Changing one here changes the pipeline. Numbers come from docs/REVIEW.md, docs/lab-notes.md and
# the README, all measured on this scope's M31 data from Belfast (mostly the 2026-09-07 night: 479 x 10 s subs).
#
# Plain shell assignments only: this file is sourced by bash.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# QA (scripts/qa_frames.py)
#   --fwhm-cap 1.5     reject only frames whose FWHM is > 1.5x the session median (absolute cap).
#   --round-min 0.75   reject elongated frames (wind, tracking) below roundness 0.75 (absolute floor).
#   --nstars-frac 0.6  reject cloud: fewer than 60 % of the local trend's star count.
#   Trend-relative FWHM/roundness culling stays OFF (no --trend-reject): it cost 12 % SNR for a 3 % FWHM gain.
#   QA always runs per night. Pooled QA reads a moonlit night's brighter sky as a "background jump" and
#   rejects the whole night.
M31_QA_ARGS="--fwhm-cap 1.5 --round-min 0.75 --nstars-frac 0.6"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deband (scripts/deband.py)
#   --axis both   check columns too. Sep 7: rows 0.14 ADU against a 0.20 ADU noise floor (skipped), columns a
#                 0.50 ADU fixed pattern at 3.3x its floor (subtracted from every frame). Correction is
#                 applied only when it is measurable; otherwise frames are hard-linked unchanged.
M31_DEBAND_ARGS="--axis both"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Synthetic flat (scripts/synth_flat.py)
#   No extra flags. With M31 masked out of the measurement the sky is flat to within 1-2 %, so the flat stage
#   reports "negligible" and run_pipeline.sh does not apply a flat. (A naive centre box read 17-22 % falloff
#   because the galaxy's own light inflated the centre.)
M31_FLAT_ARGS=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stack (scripts/stack.sh)
#   --weight nbstars   wFWHM weighting cost 17-23 % SNR for a 9 % smaller FWHM; nbstars is +11 % SNR for a
#                      4 % FWHM cost against unweighted, the best compromise on one night.
#                      (combine_sessions.sh overrides this with --weight noise --fastnorm: across nights,
#                      inverse-noise weighting keeps the moonlit night from diluting the dark ones. Sep 7 +
#                      Sep 25/26 gave +14 % SNR at fixed M31 positions over Sep 7 alone; ~15 % predicted.)
#   --ref mid          the sharpest frame sits at one end of the ~19 deg alt-az field rotation and left three
#                      corners at ~2x noise; the mid-session frame evens the coverage.
#   --framing current  the reference frame's rectangle. --framing max --maximize across nights framed at
#                      different rotations gave a ragged canvas that broke star removal and the crop.
#   --rej w            winsorized sigma clipping 3/3; rejection costs < 1 % noise.
#   --pixfrac 1.0 --scale 1.0
#                      Bayer drizzle at 1x: no demosaic interpolation. 2x drizzle bought only 3 % FWHM.
#   Per-frame --seqsubsky stays OFF: on moonlit Sep 25/26 it cut the sky tilt from 16.4 % to 1.7 % but the
#   halo/outer-disc SNR fell 15-55 % because each frame's fit absorbs galaxy light.
M31_STACK_ARGS="--weight nbstars --ref mid --framing current --rej w --pixfrac 1.0 --scale 1.0"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Post (scripts/post_process.sh, which passes the stretch flags on to scripts/stretch.py)
#   --bge subsky --subsky-degree 1
#                      a first-order polynomial flattens the sky as well as GraXpert's AI model (corner range
#                      1.2 -> 0.4 ADU vs 0.3) and keeps M31's halo; the AI model removed about half of it
#                      (3.3 -> 1.7 ADU above sky at 37' on the minor axis). Runs before colour calibration.
#   --pcc-catalog auto local Gaia when Siril points at an installed copy (works offline; VizieR was down all
#                      evening on 2026-10-16), else NOMAD. The result is corrected to the SPCC balance by
#                      scripts/cc_correct.py (factors below).
#   --denoise-strength 0.8
#                      GraXpert's strength is an exact linear blend (residual noise 0.5 / 0.8 / 1.0 of the
#                      input at 0.5 / 0.8 / 1.0). At 0.8 green sky noise drops 3.4x with no measurable loss of
#                      2-8 px structure, and a little grain stays so the sky does not look plastic.
#   --crop auto --crop-noise-ratio 1.35
#                      trim edges until each border has < 5 % blocks noisier than 1.35x the centre (clamped to
#                      1-15 % per edge). Rotated-in corners carry ~2x noise; a fixed 4 % missed them, and a
#                      per-edge median missed half-edge strips on combined nights.
#   --target-bg 0.10   darker sky, as the Seestar renders it; with sky-only smoothing the 8-bit sky noise was
#                      13 % vs the Seestar JPEG's 15 %.
#   --bg-smooth 1.0    sky-only luminance blur (stars and galaxy protected): 18 % -> 11 % sky noise.
#   --bg-desat 0.4 --bg-chroma-blur 12
#                      after PCC the blue channel carries 2.8x green's noise; grey the sky's colour speckle.
#   --knee 0.7         soft highlight shoulder; the plain stretch clipped the nucleus to a 2.1' white disc.
#   --star-reduce 0.7  star layer at 70 % of plain-stretch brightness, screen-blended over the starless galaxy.
#   --asinh 3 --saturation 1.15
#                      colour boost and chroma gain (taste, not measured).
#   Not passed on purpose: --palette (post_process.sh auto-detects FILTER='LP' and switches to hoo).
M31_POST_ARGS="--bge subsky --subsky-degree 1 --pcc-catalog auto --denoise-strength 0.8 --crop auto --crop-noise-ratio 1.35 --target-bg 0.10 --bg-smooth 1.0 --bg-desat 0.4 --bg-chroma-blur 12 --knee 0.7 --star-reduce 0.7 --asinh 3 --saturation 1.15"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Documented here, set in code (no command-line pass-through from run_pipeline.sh):
#
# Star-removal core protection (scripts/destar.py --protect-mode relative, the default)
#   Dark Star hollows out M31's nucleus, so bright, large structures are restored from the original. The old
#   absolute threshold on the stretched image (0.45) collapsed under moonlight (nucleus stretched to 0.54
#   instead of 0.93, mask peak 0.17) and left a dark ring. The relative mode protects where brightness above
#   sky is 3-9 % of the nucleus's in the linear image, so it does not depend on the sky level. It was
#   calibrated so Sep 7's mask is unchanged. Dark Star runs per colour channel as grey (< 2.5 % residual, no
#   colour blocks) with per-tile MTF normalization.
#
# PCC -> SPCC colour correction (scripts/cc_correct.py)
#   Headless SPCC segfaults in Siril 1.4.4, so PCC runs and is scaled per channel about the sky median by a
#   fixed factor measured as SPCC/PCC on the same stacks (Sony IMX585 + UV/IR Block, Average Spiral Galaxy):
#     NOMAD      R 1.520  G 1.390  B 1.000   (too blue without it: the lavender cast)
#     local Gaia R 0.720  G 0.865  B 1.000   (too red without it)
#   Oct 16 was an independent check: predicted SPCC R 0.732 G 0.583, measured 0.769 / 0.576.
#   Disable with POST_ARGS="--no-cc-correct".
#
# Multi-night crop
#   Nights imaged over different hours overlap fully only in the middle, so auto-crop can clip M31's disc.
#   The main disc (+/-60' along the major axis) fits inside the uncropped 2160 px frame width; the faint
#   outer disc (+/-80') does not. The combined result Andrew picked used a hand-made crop (sides 22/100 px,
#   top 256, bottom 243 of 2160x3840).
